Keep each compute pipeline for the next dispatch instead of compiling it again.

halide_webgpu_run creates a compute pipeline for every dispatch and releases it as soon as
the dispatch is encoded. wgpuDeviceCreateComputePipelineAsync is where an entry point is
compiled down to the adapter's own shader language, and measured in Chrome on an Apple GPU
against this project's colour kernel — one module, 64 entry points per frame — that cost
about 160 ms per dispatch, or eight to ten seconds a frame, all of it compiling pipelines the
previous frame had already compiled. The monochrome module happened to be served from a
browser-side cache and ran in tens of milliseconds; the colour module never was.

The pipelines are now kept in a list keyed by device, shader module, entry point and the four
override constants (workgroup size and workgroup memory), looked up before creating, released
with the device and at process teardown. The first frame still compiles every kernel it uses;
every later frame is a lookup. Sent nowhere yet; it belongs upstream on Halide PR #8955, which
this tree is a checkout of, and applies on top of halide-webgpu-storage-limit.patch.
